r"""AWS benchmark harness for espresso-node: fleet lifecycle, load, and query-service lag.

Runs the shared `netbench` load driver against a Terraform-provisioned fleet in one AWS account
and region, replacing manual `espresso-deploy` runs for this purpose. `plan` renders every file
and prints a cost estimate without writing anything to AWS; `run` additionally provisions,
measures, collects and destroys.

`status` shows one run (phase, instances, agent progress, cost so far) or, with `--all`, every
tagged resource in the region. `destroy DIR` tears one run down; `destroy --orphans` sweeps runs
whose local state is lost or whose TTL passed. `collect` and `render` redo those steps on an
existing run dir. `agent-drive` (on ctl) and `agent-host` (on every host) run under
uv-managed python 3.14 from /opt/bench.

Prerequisites: opentofu and awscli2 (nix devShell), an AWS profile for the benchmark account
(`--profile`, default `timeboost-dev`), an ssh key (`--ssh-key`, default `auto`: a fresh key per run, deleted after destroy), and
`--tag` naming the ghcr tag CI pushed for a `release-*` branch.
Recipes: `just bench aws plan|run|status|collect|render|destroy`.

Exit codes of `run`: 0 valid, 1 invalid result, 2 refused before any AWS write, 3 failed after
writes but destroyed, 4 resources may remain (the last log line is the destroy command; also the
exit code with `--keep`).

Examples:
    scripts/network-bench/aws-bench plan --tag release-x --offline \
        --price c8g.4xlarge=0.71 --price c8g.2xlarge=0.355
    scripts/network-bench/aws-bench plan --tag release-x --nodes 5
"""

